  "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\u222d}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(y-{x}^{2})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(xy)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(3xz) \\\\  & =(-2x)+x+3x \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={\u222d}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=1}^{x=3}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(9-1)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
